WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.669 --> 00:00:02.669
Hello there!

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:08.700
A blog post controversially entitled
“Please Commit More Blatant Academic Fraud”

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:14.660
has been making waves in the Machine Learning
/ AI community… on Twitter.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:15.660
And Reddit.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:21.340
Especially because it is the cry of a fellow
PhD student, you know, not a renowned professor

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:24.539
or anything,
Ms. Coffee Bean proposed to give this blog

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:27.960
post a read and to comment on it in this video.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.890
So what is this all about?

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:34.940
The blog post starts directly with mentioning
what event even triggered it’s writing:

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:40.719
It is this viewpoint by a renowned personality
in the Machine Learning community, Prof. Michael

00:00:40.719 --> 00:00:42.129
L. Littman.

00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:47.609
This piece is reporting about a method of
cheating the peer review called "collusion

00:00:47.609 --> 00:00:48.649
rings".

00:00:48.649 --> 00:00:53.359
And bear with Ms. Coffee Bean, because we
know that you know that peer review is broken,

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:56.260
but this is like…
organized fraud.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.550
It’s the collusion ring mafia breaking the
already broken review system.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:03.429
Okay, so what’s the deal, how does this
work?

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:08.390
Pay close attention, because this is so intricate,
that Ms. Coffee Bean honestly wonders how

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.840
somebody can even come up with this.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.030
Well, it’s smart people working in AI after
all.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:19.600
The scheme works like this:
First, a group of colluding authors…

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.030
[Google searching]
You’re not a native English speaker Ms.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.590
Coffee Bean, are you?

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:31.729
"collusion": "secret or illegal cooperation
or conspiracy in order to deceive others".

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:34.479
So we’re on that level now!

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:39.700
Ok, so a group of colluding authors writes
and submits papers to a conference.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.020
That’s something even non-crooked authors
would do.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.360
But then they share amongst themselves the
title of the papers,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.420
which is against the rules, because it ruins
anonymity in peer reviewing.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.939
Then, these colluders hide their conflict
of interest.

00:01:55.939 --> 00:02:01.190
So they hide anything that could tell a meta-reviewer
that they might know the other authors.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:02.190
How?

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:03.190
Well...

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:04.190
With duplicate accounts.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.899
And interestingly enough, there was no way
to tell these are fake accounts!?

00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:12.439
Well, not really, not in a field growing and
growing,

00:02:12.439 --> 00:02:14.400
like Machine Learning does currently.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.400
Exponentially!

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:21.040
So, the collusion ring fellowship bids for
the papers, which means that in the phase

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.670
where reviewers can tell which papers they
would like to review,

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:29.250
they choose the papers from their collusion
ring... fellowship.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:33.709
And hopefully they get the papers of their
fellows, write positive reviews to them and

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:39.200
even lobby through back channels to change
the opinions of other reviewers!?

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.349
Or send threats if others feel like something
is fishy!?

00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:44.370
Wow.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:49.760
And the most crazy part is:
This scheme seemed to work in this discovered

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.400
case, but who knows how many cases go undetected?

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.430
The viewpoint piece is not disclosing specific
details, just to respect the fraudulent researchers

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.390
and not disclose information that might identify
them.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.050
Oh, it’s anonymous peer review after all!
[mip]

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:10.659
But thinking how important conference acceptances
are for anyone, and especially for PhD students

00:03:10.659 --> 00:03:16.610
who do not have the safety of a professorship
or tenure and need to find a next job after

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:23.920
their PhD, it is no wonder it hit Jacob Buckman
– who is a PhD student – so badly, that

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.120
it motivated him to write this blog post which
we finally come to discuss right now!

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.819
Independently on whether you agree with him
or not, you are free to form your own opinions.

00:03:33.819 --> 00:03:38.569
But we think we all can agree that this is
a very grim viewpoint adopted here.

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:45.489
His main point is that this example highlighted
by Prof. Littman might be one of the worst

00:03:45.489 --> 00:03:50.850
cases of fraud,
but that there is a more “mundane, and day-to-day

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.069
fraud” that is happening regularly.

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:57.550
But if the “day-to-day fraud” would be
more blatant, like the “fellowship of the

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:02.879
collusion rings”-fraud we saw earlier, a
change in the field’s organization might

00:04:02.879 --> 00:04:07.859
come sooner, because then everybody would
see how wrong everything is and fight for

00:04:07.859 --> 00:04:08.859
a change.

00:04:08.859 --> 00:04:10.939
So what exactly is wrong in ML?

00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:16.269
Examples of this “day-to-day fraud” are:
We cite: “Trying that shiny new algorithm

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:20.790
out on a couple dozen seeds, and then only
reporting the best few."

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:21.790
Well...

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:23.020
That's happening.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:24.020
For sure.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:28.860
"Running a big hyperparameter sweep on your
proposed approach but using the defaults for

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:29.860
the baseline."

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:35.920
Who even spends so much time tuning the baselines
and some of the baselines are not even tunable

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.160
because the code was not released or it's
not working for some reason.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.780
"Cherry-picking examples where your model
looks good, or cherry-picking whole datasets

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:48.380
to test on, where you’ve confirmed your
model’s advantage."

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:49.380
Mhmm..

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:54.540
"Making up new problem settings, new datasets,
new objectives in order to claim victory on

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.400
an empty playing field.”

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.670
Well, this one is a little against the goal
of research, and has been discussed on Twitter.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:06.380
And Ms. Coffee Bean also thinks it's a little
unfair, because even if sometimes you don’t

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:11.860
see the impact of a new problem or application,
there are people who genuinely need that,

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:16.550
it’s not necessarily that an author is creating
a problem just for the sake of having one.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:22.030
Okay then “Proclaiming that your work is
a “promising first step” in your introduction,

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:26.420
despite being fully aware that nobody will
ever build on it.

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:29.900
Submitting a paper to a conference because
it’s got a decent shot at acceptance and

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:35.220
you don’t want the time you spent on it
go to waste, even though you’ve since realized

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.910
that the core ideas aren’t quite correct.”

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:44.620
This last point is very heavily weighing on
PhD students with limited time to get to hold

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.440
on to something.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.210
Especially if things are not going so well,
there is a lot of pressure to still make a

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:52.890
publication out of that.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:59.480
This is why, Ms. Coffee bean salutes initiatives
that highlight negative results in the field,

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:05.440
like this one here, making it unnecessary
to hide that results are bad and to mask them

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:06.540
as actually good.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:12.240
Okay, so these are the problems, and Jacob
Buckman (Ms. Coffee Bean, is this how the

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.100
name is really pronounced?)

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:20.330
So Jacob writes that the even bigger problem
is that these points are subtle and easy to

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:26.220
dismiss by saying something like the hyperparameters
were just forgotten, or that something is

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:31.250
just a bug,
therefore nobody can propose hard punishments

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:32.250
for this.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:37.600
And Ms. Coffee Bean’s “rule of cheating”
says that if one does it, the next does it,

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:43.090
until most of people do it just to keep up
with everybody who is cheating already.

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:48.510
Ok, but the next point in the post is saying
that everybody is complicit, because nobody

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:51.380
acknowledges the existence of this subtle
fraud.

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:56.410
And there are some reasons to this, especially
those people benefiting from the current state

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:59.680
of affairs, do not want a new organization
of things.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:01.560
That's easy to understand.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:06.440
But this point falls rather short in the blog
post, Ms. Coffee Bean,doesn’t it?

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:11.190
Because this point implies a little that everybody
knows about these problems.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:12.440
But do they?

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:16.880
Here we might have to split between people
who do know, and people who don’t.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.900
There are some examples of people who don’t:
My experience from teaching students at my

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:27.400
university is that people are not born knowing
about these problems, but they have to learn

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.840
about them, one by one.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:34.280
Especially at Bachelors and even Masters level,
it is very common that research papers are

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:35.860
read like the Bible.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:41.160
Things in it must be true if it stands in
a paper, it was accepted at a conference!

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.940
Someone even formatted it nicely with LaTeX!

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:47.680
Critical thinking is something that one has
to learn.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:53.060
Another group of people who do not know about
the problems, but have critical thinking are

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:55.280
experienced researchers.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:00.790
Critical thinking applies only to their fields
of expertise, because on some topics they

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:03.630
do not know enough to even think critically!

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:08.200
And you know, if you are just visiting a field
or topic, you will also not invest the time

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:10.030
necessary to learn ALL things.

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:11.440
You're just passing by!

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:16.330
Okay, but even if Ms. Coffee Bean does not
agree with distributing the blame so widely,

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:21.870
she does agree that there are not many people
doing something effectively about the problems,

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.170
even if there are some attempts at it.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:28.400
Some attempts were highlighted in this tweet
by Anna Rogers, linked in the description

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:29.400
below.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:30.400
Check it out!

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:33.669
But what to do about these problems int the
proposal of this blog post?

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:39.409
Well, the proposal is to make academic fraud
blatant, visible to everyone but especially

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:45.430
unacceptable so that one cannot merely stand
aside doing nothing about it.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:48.279
“Make blatant fraud, aggressive fraud.

00:08:48.279 --> 00:08:52.180
Form more collusion rings!” like the ones
we have seen in the beginning.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:58.170
This is the grim call of a PhD student and
this has to be taken seriously.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:05.480
He even makes an analogy to chemotherapy;
unpleasant, but effective, even if it destroys

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:06.480
the field entirely.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.230
So Ms. Coffee Bean’s question is, do we
want this to happen to the Machine Learning

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:12.230
community?

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:16.639
Do we want to kill it to hope it rises again
but without all problems from before?

00:09:16.639 --> 00:09:22.200
Well, as someone originally from a country
that has recently seen this kind of treatment

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:28.360
in a bloody revolution, but where at the end
all the old problems came back in a slightly

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:34.430
different form, this time just dressed as
capitalism instead of socialism, I am very

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:40.660
distrustful that even if everybody would follow
this advice, this would really cure the problems.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:43.029
Because who would follow this advice?

00:09:43.029 --> 00:09:46.120
Who would volunteer to make these blatant
frauds?

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:51.910
Anyone willing to put their academic integrity
on the line?

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:52.910
No?

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:53.910
Anyone?

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:57.910
Do not get us wrong here, I as a fellow PhD
student, love the revolutionary, angry tone

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:00.351
of this blog post, I feel with it.

00:10:00.351 --> 00:10:05.730
It is great that these to pics come up as
a discussion and here we cheer for Jacob who

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:06.800
wrote it.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:07.800
[cheering]

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:13.670
But Ms. Coffee Bean takes me down from my
angry PhD frustrations and tells me to think

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:19.110
more constructively and propose a solution
that not only has a chance to work, but one

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:22.410
that people are even willing to apply in the
first place.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:29.329
So here it is, the long-awaited solution to
all problems in ML publications, reviewing

00:10:29.329 --> 00:10:31.290
and the collusion ring cancer!

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:33.089
Are you ready?

00:10:33.089 --> 00:10:34.680
[Flat line]

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:40.810
Sorry, the broadcast was interrupted, just
like during the Romanian revolution in 1989.

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:46.160
Ah, that's the reference probably not many
viewers will get!

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:47.160
Sorry!

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:49.610
Okay, Ms. Coffee Bean has three proposals.

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:54.630
They are not fully fleshed out, that might
possibly become a future video…

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:55.630
I don't know!

00:10:55.630 --> 00:10:59.550
Here they are, in their current form:
The first thing is education.

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:05.579
As previously said from my teaching experience,
there is a lot of work needed to teach students

00:11:05.579 --> 00:11:10.339
(and people in general), that just because
something is written in a conference format,

00:11:10.339 --> 00:11:15.240
or is, I don't know: in a nice video, does
not make it true.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:20.759
Critical thinking (also useful for reviewing)
must be learnt and practiced, not taken for

00:11:20.759 --> 00:11:21.759
granted.

00:11:21.759 --> 00:11:26.370
And the best thing that could happen out of
this, is that critically thinking students

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:30.329
will take over, when the dinosaurs conducting
science badly, will disappear.

00:11:30.329 --> 00:11:35.070
I know, it is a long-time investment, nobody
likes these.

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:38.009
The second proposal is: Discussion.

00:11:38.009 --> 00:11:42.540
Putting these issues into public view and
to everyone’s discussion, exactly like this

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:43.540
post does.

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:47.829
Ms. Coffee Bean loves it how it highlights
these subtle problems and puts them into broad

00:11:47.829 --> 00:11:52.990
daylight for people all around the world to
discuss about this on Twitter or Reddit.

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:55.459
And the final idea is…

00:11:55.459 --> 00:11:59.749
In-depth and independent science journalism
in Machine Learning.

00:11:59.749 --> 00:12:06.829
Writers, bloggers, or ahem, even people with
YouTube channels to get monthly a random subset

00:12:06.829 --> 00:12:13.190
of all new publications to critically assess,
publicly review and simultaneously communicate

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:16.209
to non-experts what the field is up to.

00:12:16.209 --> 00:12:21.199
The upside: Even if not all publications from
that month will fall into this random selective

00:12:21.199 --> 00:12:24.749
lottery,
there is a chance that when an author publishes

00:12:24.749 --> 00:12:29.059
a paper, that paper will be randomly chosen
for the public review process.

00:12:29.059 --> 00:12:34.311
It’s enough of an incentive for the authors
to make their paper waterproof, especially

00:12:34.311 --> 00:12:39.889
knowing that a well-known critic will say
something about your paper.

00:12:39.889 --> 00:12:44.290
Of course one has to take care that these
public reviewers do not have any conflict

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:48.730
of interest or that they have to be afraid
that their own papers would get a negative

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:53.790
review, so what Ms. Coffee Bean is imagining
is this as a full-time job for them, like

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:56.999
literary critics for novels or for poetry.

00:12:56.999 --> 00:13:02.399
And of course, these journalists / reviewers
should be well paid, perhaps by someone with

00:13:02.399 --> 00:13:06.699
a lot of money caring for a healthy and diverse
field;

00:13:06.699 --> 00:13:13.319
to create perhaps a common fond, where any
ML benefactor could contribute to.

00:13:13.319 --> 00:13:14.379
What do you think of this?

00:13:14.379 --> 00:13:18.699
Of course, these are just some suggestions
Ms. Coffee Bean thought of just now.

00:13:18.699 --> 00:13:24.079
So let’s discuss: Write in the comments,
tweet or perhaps write a position paper for

00:13:24.079 --> 00:13:25.339
the next conference.

00:13:25.339 --> 00:13:29.129
How should we improve the state of publication
in AI?

00:13:29.129 --> 00:13:31.389
Perhaps without killing it first, ok?

00:13:31.389 --> 00:13:33.899
So tell us what do you think.

00:13:33.899 --> 00:13:36.949
Ms. Coffee Bean will devour your comments.

00:13:36.949 --> 00:13:42.670
Even if the state of ML academia is not healthy,
we hope you are.

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:45.020
Stay safe.

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:49.099
Okay bye!

